---
title: "Planning, fetching and sharding"
---

```{r setup, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", fig.path = "figures/pfs-",
                      fig.width = 7, fig.height = 5, dpi = 96)
options(width = 90)
```

Every zaro read goes through three steps you can also run yourself:

    selection --plan--> refs table --fetch--> raw bytes --decode--> array

`zaro_plan()` says which bytes a read needs before anything is read;
`zaro_fetch()` gets them; `zaro_read()` and `zaro_array()` do both and
decode. The plan is the same shape whatever the store: a native Zarr chunk
is a whole object, a virtual chunk is a byte range in a NetCDF file, a
sharded chunk is a byte range in a shard object.

## A plan

```{r plan}
library(zaro)
options(zaro.verbose = FALSE)

bran <- zaro("virtualizarr://https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mdsumner/virtualized/refs/heads/main/remote/ocean_temp_2023.parq")

## the surface (st_ocean 0) on one day: 60 chunks of 300 x 300
p <- zaro_plan(bran, "temp", start = c(0, 0, 0, 0), count = c(1, 1, NA, NA))
p
```

The columns follow the [byterefs](https://github.com/byterefs/spec) view:
`dim_0..dim_3` (the chunk's grid index), `present`, `path`, `offset`,
`size` and `data` (inline bytes), then zaro's `key` and `transport`.

```{r plan-cols}
names(p)
head(as.data.frame(p)[c("dim_2", "dim_3", "offset", "size", "transport")])
```

It is a plain data frame: filter it, save it, or hand it to another tool.

## Fetching

`zaro_fetch()` sends every HTTP row through one curl connection pool, at
most `max_con` connections per host. Byte ranges that touch in the same
file are merged into one request. This slice's 60 chunks sit back to back
in the NetCDF file:

```{r fetch}
raw <- zaro_fetch(bran, p)
attr(raw, "stats")
```

Merging cuts requests, at the cost of parallelism; `options(zaro.merge_gap)`
widens what counts as touching, and `options(zaro.max_con)` sets the pool
size.

## Sharded Zarr V3

In a sharded array, each stored object (a shard) packs many inner chunks,
with an index of where each one is. zaro plans a shard as one more level of
byte references: it reads the shard's index with one small ranged request,
then each inner chunk is a byte range of the shard.

This is a Copernicus EOPF Sentinel-2 L2A product published as sharded
GeoZarr: each 10 m band is one 10980 x 10980 shard of 45 x 45 inner chunks.

```{r eopf}
eopf <- zaro("https://s3.explorer.eopf.copernicus.eu/esa-zarr-sentinel-explorer-fra/tests-output/sentinel-2-l2a/S2C_MSIL2A_20260120T084301_N0511_R064_T36UXA_20260120T122910.zarr")
b04 <- zaro_meta(eopf, "measurements/reflectance/r10m/b04")
b04

## a 1000 x 1000 window: 25 inner chunks
pw <- zaro_plan(eopf, "measurements/reflectance/r10m/b04",
                start = c(5000, 5000), count = c(1000, 1000), meta = b04)
pw
```

```{r eopf-read}
v <- zaro_read(eopf, "measurements/reflectance/r10m/b04",
               start = c(5000, 5000), count = c(1000, 1000), meta = b04)
image(t(v[nrow(v):1, ]), col = grey.colors(64), asp = 1, axes = FALSE,
      main = "Sentinel-2 B04 reflectance, 1000 x 1000 window")
```

zaro's sharded reads are checked against zarr-python and GDAL on this
store, against GDAL's sharding test fixtures, and against arrays written
by zarr-python.
